note bottom of REQUEST
  The later request approved preparation
  and publication without changing old flags.
  Its narrow scope excludes operator downloads,
  builds and training, so old projections
  cannot expand the original grant.
end note

note bottom of FREEZE
  The source gate rejects unreviewed native
  filenames before preparation can copy them.
  Hash comparison also rejects changed contents,
  because a stable filename alone cannot bind
  the reviewed source generation.
end note

note bottom of PACKAGER
  Explicit archive membership prevents recursive
  packaging from adding unrecorded context files.
  The workspace payload budget is a soft cap,
  not a filesystem quota or proof of
  complete offline build coverage.
end note

note bottom of PUBLISHER
  The publisher runs outside bounded workers
  so existing auth stays available.
  Permission checks and frozen callbacks consume
  the same shrinking publication budget as
  each attempted mutation, without restarting it.
end note

note bottom of MANIFEST
  The asset manifest names the pushed commit,
  so creating it earlier would cause a cycle.
  Writing it into ignored cache after publication
  of source avoids changing that commit
  or the frozen bundle.
end note

note bottom of ATTEMPTS
  Recording attempts before mutation preserves
  counts when command capture fails.
  An attempted commit stays unknown until
  success is confirmed, because missing evidence
  cannot establish a negative outcome.
end note

note bottom of BYTE_CHECK
  The byte check qualifies only its captured
  snapshot, not later spec edits.
  Its three index comparisons therefore remain
  historical evidence, while the publisher still
  checks frozen inputs before mutation.
end note

note bottom of PUBLICATION_STOP
  The stopped publication consumes its attempt,
  even though no mutation command was invoked.
  Zero invocation counts preserve that
  distinction; they cannot authorize another
  publication or a transport change.
end note

note bottom of NETWORK_TARGETS
  Origin metadata identifies the repository,
  but it does not select network transport.
  The fixed network target and verified commit
  keep the push independent of remote aliases
  and later changes to the branch head.
end note

note bottom of PRIVATE_QUERIES
  Expanded origin values and rewrite records
  can contain credentials that receipts should
  not retain.
  Private queries return only decisions and
  counts, without changing configuration.
end note

note bottom of REUSE
  Publisher edits require a new source gate,
  not another copy of unchanged native inputs.
  The lineage check preserves old payload
  identities while binding current publisher
  code to a separate reviewed generation.
end note

note bottom of IDENTITY_FIELDS
  Parent checks recorded four presence flags,
  not identity values or a validated author.
  The old child omitted those fields, so the fix
  preserves existing identity rather than
  inventing a replacement.
end note

note bottom of OUTCOME_CHECK
  The retained receipt keeps its null commit
  outcome rather than rewriting earlier evidence.
  A separate check records unchanged HEAD,
  so invocation consumption remains distinct
  from creation of a new commit.
end note

note bottom of PRIVATE_IDENTITY
  The private check uses the same child context
  that will run the commit with inherited fields.
  Its publication clock leaves no separate budget,
  and the callback returns only a Boolean result,
  not raw author or committer output.
end note

